-- examples/checks/provenance-relational.rofl — PROVENANCE IS A RELATION.
--
-- START.md section 7 forbids `Provenance as a log string` and section 4 promises
-- that `On every successful rule firing the kernel emits` it. A log string can
-- be printed and cannot be JOINED, so the probe is a join: the middle column of
-- a firing is a rule id that `rule[$kernel]` also holds, and the relation that
-- firing concluded into comes back through `concludes[$kernel]`. Three-way,
-- inside the language, with no host help.
--
-- WHY EVERY HEAD HERE PROJECTS THE FACT ID AWAY. Reading provenance is
-- self-feeding: `w(F, R, T) :- derived_by[$kernel](F, R, T).` does not
-- terminate, because firing `w` emits a row that matches `w`'s own premise and
-- the fact ids never repeat. That is not a defect on its face -- it is what
-- `emits on every firing` MEANS, seen from inside -- but it is not a check.
-- Projected onto the rule, or onto the rule and the tick, or onto the relation
-- concluded into, the answer is finite: there are finitely many rules,
-- finitely many ticks, finitely many relations, so each new firing lands on a
-- row that is already there and the fixpoint closes.
--
-- `d` is derivable TWO ways on purpose. A kernel that emitted one row per FACT
-- rather than one per FIRING would move this world's census.

edb(seed).
seed(1).
seed(2).

b(X) :- seed(X).
c(X) :- b(X).
d(X) :- b(X).
d(X) :- c(X).

fired_rule(R)   :- derived_by[$kernel](_, R, _), rule[$kernel](R).
fired_into(Rel) :- derived_by[$kernel](_, R, _), concludes[$kernel](R, Rel).
fired_at(R, T)  :- derived_by[$kernel](_, R, T).
